{"type":"text","timestamp":1795795256177,"sessionID":"ses_de2434bf1ffe9e34464c344Trd","part":{"id":"prt_21dbcfb3e001vR4TEYdqKjfnKm","sessionID":"ses_de2434bf1ffe9e34464c344Trd","messageID":"msg_21dbcdd6f0013JLUe1qGRkU2ku","type":"text","text":"I reviewed both files. They are small, so the honest assessment is that naming is mostly conventional, with a couple of minor concrete issues.\n\n## Findings\n\n**main.go**\n\n1. **Non-descriptive parameter names `a` and `b`** (line 3). Single-letter identifiers convey no meaning beyond \"two integers.\" For a general addition helper this is a common math convention and acceptable, but `left`/`right` or `first`/`second` (or `x`/`y`) would be more self-documenting. This is the only real naming issue.\n\n2. **`add` is appropriately named** for an unexported helper in `package main`. No change needed. Renaming to `Add` would be wrong here: there is no external consumer, and exporting it would be misleading.\n\n**main_test.go**\n\n1. **`TestAdd` follows Go convention correctly** (`Test` + capitalized function under test). No issue.\n2. **Parameter `t *testing.T`** is the standard idiomatic name. No issue.\n\n## Not naming issues (noted so they are not confused with the above)\n\n- The duplicated call `add(2, 3)` in the test (lines 6 and 7) is repetition, not a naming problem.\n- `package main` and `func main()` are correct for an executable.\n\n## Summary\n\nOnly one concrete naming item is worth considering: the generic `a`/`b` parameter names in `main.go`. Everything else already follows Go naming conventions. No files were modified.","time":{"start":1795795254078,"end":1795795256177}}}
